% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% The nearest competitive-setting prior work. LLM-coded agents against human
% students in a repeated market/routing tournament. Humans took the top five
% places; the best LLM agent placed 6th; 33 of 40 LLM agents lost to trivial
% baselines; asking GPT-5 Pro to improve the winning student's code cost it
% nine places. One-shot generation with prompting strategies -- no iterative
% loop against an opponent pool, no Elo, no percentile, no RL baseline.
% This is our motivation, not our counterexample: it is evidence that the
% model alone does not close the gap, which is what makes the loop the claim.
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
@article{vibecoding2025tournament,
  title  = {Can Vibe Coding Beat Graduate {CS} Students? An {LLM} vs.\ Human
            Coding Tournament on Market-driven Strategic Planning},
  author = {Danassis, Panayiotis and Goel, Naman},
  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2511.20613},
  year   = {2025},
  note   = {38{,}304 matches, 40 LLM-coded agents vs.\ 17 human-coded agents}
}

% Verified against the HTML full text 2026-08-18, because the introduction had
% described this paper wrongly in a way that flattered our argument. Facts, so
% nobody has to re-derive them:
%   - humans were GRADUATE students ("human(graduate students)-coded agents"),
%     not undergraduates. The archive's contestants are a different population;
%     do not let the two swap wording.
%   - 38,304 matches (3192 x 12 double all-play-all), 112 per agent. The
%     abstract rounds this to ~40k; the exact figure is in the body.
%   - humans took the top 5 spots; 33 of 40 LLM agents lost to very simple
%     baselines; GPT-5 Pro given the winning student program placed 10th.
%   - NOT single-shot, which the intro asserted for several drafts. 5 prompting
%     strategies x 4 models x 2 runs: A1/A2/GEN are one generation pass, but IR
%     (self-play scores fed back) and CR (GPT-5 critique) each add a revision
%     round -- 16 of 40 agents. On top of that ALL 40 went through an unbounded
%     human-in-the-loop debug cycle, "e.g., 5-15" prompt-with-error rounds, and
%     the authors note they had to generate extra agents to get 40 bug-free
%     ones. Student code got no such treatment; crashes scored as losses.
% What survives, and what the intro now says instead: the revision they do is
% one round on a SELF-PLAY signal the authors themselves call "weak, compared to
% iterative refinement in other coding benchmarks", the debug loop optimizes
% compilability rather than strength, and no agent was revised against the
% tournament. Our claim is about many revisions carrying an adversarial signal.
% Do not restore "generates code once" -- it is false and it is not needed.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Load-bearing for the upper bound. VERIFIED against the v2 full text
% 2026-08-18 (arXiv:2603.24755v2, 7 May 2026, 26pp). The annotation here
% previously carried v1 numbers, every one of which v2 changed; the intro
% inherited them. Trust only this block, and re-verify if a v3 appears.
%
% Scale: 36 problems, 196 checkpoints, 15 coding agents across six providers.
% (NOT 20/93/11 -- that was v1.) Two-hour wall clock per run, no turn or cost
% cap, Python only. Best agent passes 14.8% of checkpoints; no configuration
% passes every checkpoint of any problem.
%
% Degradation: structural erosion (concentrated complexity) rises in 77% of
% agent trajectories, verbosity (redundant code) in 75.5%. Against 473
% open-source Python repos, agent code is 2.3x more verbose and 2.0x more
% eroded; per checkpoint, agent verbosity grows 6.6x and erosion 5.0x faster.
% Cost per checkpoint grows 2.2x first-to-last while relative lines changed
% fall 97.4% -> 29.5%; RQ1's own wording is that cost "rises without
% corresponding improvements to correctness".
%
% Do NOT write that human repos "flatten". They degrade too: 68% of repos with
% five or more sampled commits end more verbose (median growth 36%), erosion
% rises in 53%, per-checkpoint slopes 0.0022 verbosity / 0.0053 erosion against
% 0.0144 / 0.0264 for agents. The finding is a RATE gap, not exemption, and the
% rate gap is the comparison the upper bound actually wants.
%
% Prompting (their 3.4): anti-slop and plan-first vs a just-solve baseline.
% Verbosity down 27.5-35.6% and erosion down 34.3-57.6% depending on model, but
% "little impact on the iterative degradation" -- and it costs 2.4/3.6 pp of
% strict pass rate (2.3 pp averaged, their contribution list) plus 12.1% cost
% per checkpoint. Only on one model (GPT 5.4) does quality improve start-to-end
% at all.
%
% Do NOT write "the slope is statistically unchanged" or "statistically
% indistinguishable". There is no significance test, p-value, CI, or regression
% on the prompting experiment anywhere in the paper; the words "slope" and
% "intercept" appear once each, in Appendix D, about the human panel. The
% defensible claim is that the prompts move the starting level without slowing
% the growth rate, which is all the capacity-limit argument needs.
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
@article{slopcodebench2026,
  title  = {SlopCodeBench: Benchmarking How Coding Agents Degrade Over
            Long-Horizon Iterative Tasks},
  author = {Orlanski, Gabriel and Roy, Devjeet and Yun, Alexander and
            Shin, Changho and Gu, Alex and Ge, Albert and Adila, Dyah and
            Roberts, Nicholas and Sala, Frederic and Albarghouthi, Aws},
  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2603.24755},
  year   = {2026},
  note   = {SlopCodeBench; v2: 36 problems, 196 checkpoints, 15 agents}
}

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% The quantitative anchor for maintenance capacity, and mechanistically closer
% than SlopCodeBench: successful agentic trajectories on SWE-bench Verified stay
% under 20k-30k tokens, and longer accumulated context correlates with lower
% resolve rates. Padding context to 64k with guaranteed perfect retrieval makes
% it worse, not better (Qwen3-Coder-30B-A3B 7%, GPT-5-nano 0 tasks), which rules
% out retrieval failure as the explanation. Failure modes are hallucinated diffs
% and wrong file targets -- edits that damage working code, which is the
% signature our plateau should show.
%
% CAUTION, and it must be stated in the text: their axis is accumulated
% *context*, ours is program *description length*. Related, not identical. Use
% this as an independent order-of-magnitude check on \val{maintcap} after
% converting units, never as though it measured the same thing. If our measured
% capacity lands near their threshold that is convergent evidence worth naming;
% if it does not, the difference is a result, not an error.
%
% VERIFIED against the v2 full text 2026-08-18. All figures above are correct
% (v2, 6 Mar 2026; ICLR 2026 ICBINB workshop). One structural caveat the intro
% had flattened, now fixed: the 64k run is SINGLE-SHOT patch generation, on 100
% BM25-plus-gold-patch samples from SWE-bench Verified, using two models
% (Qwen3-Coder-30B-A3B 7%, GPT-5-nano 0) that are not the same set as the
% agentic run. So it is not a controlled "add context to the agent" comparison
% -- it confounds long context with the loss of decomposition, and writing it as
% "padding the context makes matters worse" overstates what they did.
% Their own framing is the one to use, and it serves us better: agentic success
% "primarily arises from task decomposition into short-context steps rather than
% effective long-context reasoning", and failures "persist even under perfect
% retrieval". That is why the citation supports "not a retrieval limit" -- recall
% was guaranteed at 100% within the single-shot condition -- and why the useful
% consequence for us is that a program under revision cannot be decomposed into
% short-context steps the way a repair trajectory can.
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
@inproceedings{longcontextbugfix2026,
  title     = {The Limits of Long-Context Reasoning in Automated Bug Fixing},
  author    = {Raju, Ravi and Ji, Mengmeng and Upasani, Shubhangi and
               Li, Bo and Thakker, Urmish},
  booktitle = {ICLR Workshop on I Can't Believe It's Not Better},
  year      = {2026},
  note      = {arXiv:2602.16069}
}

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Why accumulation degrades revision quality, isolated from context length.
% Given the knowledge and plan up front -- so reasoning is not the variable --
% per-step accuracy still falls as steps accumulate, and models become more
% likely to err when the context already contains their own earlier errors.
% Self-conditioning is not fixed by scaling parameters; thinking mitigates it.
%
% This is the mechanism our append-only log can test directly: near the plateau,
% do failed revisions regress already-working behaviour rather than merely add
% nothing?
%
% VERIFIED against the v3 full text 2026-08-18 (arXiv:2509.09677v3, ICLR 2026),
% which changed two things we were asserting and added one we should be using.
%
% 1. Scaling does not merely fail to remove self-conditioning -- "scaling model
%    size increases self-conditioning", their Figure 5. Our claim was weaker
%    than the source for once; it is now stated at full strength.
% 2. "Thinking fixes self-conditioning" (their Result 5): Qwen3 thinking models
%    do not self-condition at all, and CoT alone does not substitute (Fig 17).
%    CONCEDE THIS IN THE TEXT. It is a caveat -- our agent reasons -- but it
%    cuts our way: if reasoning removes self-conditioning, a plateau we observe
%    with a reasoning agent cannot be waved away as self-conditioning, so the
%    concession strengthens the capacity reading rather than weakening it.
% 3. The claim to lean on instead is their Result 7: benchmarked on task length
%    executable in a single turn, frontier reasoning models are finite and far
%    apart -- GPT-5 2176 steps, Claude-4 Sonnet 432, Grok 4 384, Gemini 2.5 Pro
%    120. A finite, sharply model-dependent horizon IS maintenance capacity as a
%    property of the agent. Do not convert their steps into our description
%    length; the units are their synthetic task's, same caution as
%    longcontextbugfix2026.
%
% Also real: self-conditioning appears in realistic agentic benchmarks, roughly
% 20% of GAIA, 48% of ALFWorld and 33% of WebShop failures resembling it
% (their Appendix A, on annotated GPT-4o trajectories -- a non-thinking model,
% so consistent with point 2).
%
% REFEREE RISK, and know it before one raises it: this paper's headline argues
% AGAINST diminishing returns -- marginal per-step gains compound into
% exponentially longer horizons, and it closes on "the massive benefits of
% scaling model size and sequential test-time compute". We cite it for a
% mechanism it establishes, not for its thesis. That is legitimate, but never
% paraphrase the paper as though it reported a pessimistic result, and if a
% referee raises the tension the answer is that a growing horizon is still a
% finite one, which is all the upper bound requires.
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
@inproceedings{longhorizonexecution2026,
  title     = {The Illusion of Diminishing Returns: Measuring Long Horizon
               Execution in {LLM}s},
  author    = {Sinha, Akshit and Arun, Arvindh and Goel, Shashwat and
               Staab, Steffen and Geiping, Jonas},
  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},
  year      = {2026},
  note      = {arXiv:2509.09677}
}

