% budget (B = n x d <= 256, n in {1,2,4,8,16}, 10-task subset).
%
% Do NOT write "flattens within tens of iterations", which the intro did until
% 2026-08-18. The majority of improvement events fall in the first ~30 steps,
% but there is a long tail out to iteration 500 and their own summary puts
% marginal returns near zero "after ~50-100 iterations". "Tens" also picks the
% wrong shape: what they report is a DOUBLE squeeze -- rarer AND smaller
% improvements -- not a plateau. Cite the pair of exponents; it is a stronger
% concession and it gives our own decay a shape to be compared against rather
% than a single number.
%
% Three differences to state in the text: no human or RL baselines, budget
% counted in evaluator interactions only, and a single agent against a fixed
% verifier -- so its score is absolute and monotone, where Elo against a pool
% is neither. (An earlier version of this note said "drifting pool", which
% contradicts the paper's own frozen-pool invariant. Our test pool is FROZEN;
% what makes Elo non-monotone is that it is relative to a population, not that
% the population moves. The archive's human field drifts across editions, which
% is a different matter entirely -- see 02's scope note.) Match its fitting
% procedure so \val{decay} is comparable, including the constrained-slope fit.
